item.text:
Searches covered German and EU parliamentary records, arms-control research, Mali training oversight, recipient monitoring, and the April list-editing allegation. They produced useful evidence of weak Ukraine end-use verification, a documented German-backed remedy in Mali, substantial inability to trace EUTM trainees to later abuses, and a primary government record confirming the Saudi decision and its formal review. No reliable source independently tied German-supplied weapons to specific civilian harm in Ukraine, identified German-trained personnel among the Moura perpetrators, documented corrective action following verified misuse of German weapons in Ukraine, or resolved who edited the April industry list. The Saudi record establishes a Bundessicherheitsrat decision, but does not disclose Scholz’s individual position or vote.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Arms Trade Bulletin March–April 2022: End-use monitoring of arms transfers to Ukraine","publisher":"International Peace Information Service (IPIS)","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://ipisresearch.be/weekly-briefing/arms-trade-bulletin-march-april-2022/","claim":"Independent arms-control researchers found that Germany had obtained Ukrainian undertakings permitting possible post-shipment checks, but German authorities had not conducted any on-site verification in Ukraine as of May 2022. They concluded that it was unknown whether exporting states were effectively monitoring weapons during the full-scale war, including for diversion and serious human-rights or humanitarian-law violations.","locator":"Paragraphs 77-92, especially 85-92; source note 3 records IPIS communication with the German Federal Foreign Office dated 7 May 2022.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"scholz_2022_ukraine_end_use_monitoring_not_verified","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_research","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist independent organization states the implementation finding expressly and identifies its direct communication with the German Foreign Office; it distinguishes promised safeguards from actual verification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct contemporaneous assessment in May 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level implementation gap under Scholz; the source does not show that Scholz personally designed or obstructed monitoring.","contrary_evidence":"Germany had formal end-use undertakings, prior-consent requirements for re-export, and permission for possible post-shipment checks, but those safeguards had not been independently tested through German on-site inspection.","underlying_fact_key":"ukraine_2022_german_end_use_control_effectiveness","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Monitoring of arms deliveries to Ukraine since January 2022 – Fact Sheet – December 2022","publisher":"Groupe de recherche et d'information sur la paix et la sécurité (GRIP)","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.grip.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/2022-12-20-Fact-Sheet-UKR-January-November-2022.pdf","claim":"GRIP warned that the speed and scale of 2022 arms transfers to Ukraine did not allow adequate anticipation of diversion risks; EU and US mitigation measures were only beginning, and their effectiveness remained uncertain. This is a system-wide risk assessment, not a finding that German weapons were actually diverted or misused.","locator":"Section 4 concluding discussion, p. 9, PDF lines 329-337.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"ukraine_2022_rapid_transfers_diversion_controls_uncertain","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5"],"source_type":"independent_research","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous specialist fact sheet clearly separates identified monitoring weaknesses from proof of actual diversion.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Assesses deliveries from January through November 2022 and was published in December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Context for recipient-risk management by Scholz’s government; no personal Scholz attribution and no Germany-specific misuse finding.","contrary_evidence":"The report establishes serious untested risk, but expressly does not establish diversion, civilian harm, or German governmental tolerance of verified misuse.","underlying_fact_key":"ukraine_2022_german_end_use_control_effectiveness","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Federal Government answer concerning arms exports to Saudi Arabia despite expiration of the Yemen truce","publisher":"Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Climate Action / German Federal Government","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://www.bundeswirtschaftsministerium.de/Redaktion/DE/Parlamentarische-Anfragen/2022/12/20-4465.pdf?__blob=publicationFile&v=4","claim":"The government’s formal parliamentary answer confirms that the Saudi exports followed a final Bundessicherheitsrat decision identified in the question as dated 27 September 2022. It states that the September licences were examined under the applicable legal framework, including all eight criteria of EU Common Position 2008/944/CFSP, but does not disclose the substantive country-specific assessment or Scholz’s individual position.","locator":"Answer to question 12, pp. 10-11; joint answer to questions 16-17, pp. 12-13. The underlying notification is identified as Ausschussdrucksache 20(9)149, 27 September 2022.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"saudi_2022_bundessicherheitsrat_final_export_decision","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"primary_government_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"This is the government’s formal answer and supplies the primary parliamentary trail to the final Bundessicherheitsrat decision; secrecy still prevents individual-vote attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct record of the September 2022 approval process.","ruler_attribution":"The decision is attributable to the Scholz government and Bundessicherheitsrat. The record does not prove Scholz’s personal vote, instruction, or unilateral approval.","contrary_evidence":"Formal criteria review occurred, including human-rights, humanitarian-law, and regional-stability criteria; the government did not publish the underlying reasoning or explain how the identified recipient risks were overcome.","underlying_fact_key":"saudi_2022_bundessicherheitsrat_approval","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Federal Government answer on Mali missions and human-rights abuses","publisher":"German Federal Government / Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://dserver.bundestag.de/btd/20/022/2002215.pdf","claim":"After reports of the Moura killings, the government said it demanded that Mali permit an independent investigation and that, at Germany’s urging, all EUTM training for formed Malian army and National Guard units was suspended until further notice. It separately stated that Germany pressed Mali for investigation and prosecution of other well-founded abuse allegations.","locator":"Answer to question 5, pp. 2-3, PDF lines 98-111; answer to question 6, p. 3, lines 112-121; answer to question 11, p. 5, lines 176-186.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"germany_2022_mali_abuse_response_training_suspension","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.9"],"source_type":"primary_government_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Formal parliamentary answer specifies the remedial measures and expressly attributes the suspension initiative to the German government, though it is not independent verification of German motives or implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Records actions taken immediately after the March 2022 Moura operation and April 2022 EU decision.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level remedial response under Scholz; no evidence that Scholz personally ordered or managed the response.","contrary_evidence":"The suspension was preventive and based on grave allegations and insufficient safeguards; the record did not establish that EUTM-trained personnel committed the abuses.","underlying_fact_key":"mali_2022_eutm_suspension_after_abuse_risk","lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Federal Government answer on traceability and operational monitoring of EUTM Mali trainees","publisher":"German Federal Government / Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://dserver.bundestag.de/btd/20/022/2002215.pdf","claim":"The government could not identify whether personnel implicated in reported killings had previously received EUTM or EUCAP training. It had no quantitative breakdown of which of roughly 16,000 EUTM-trained Malian personnel were trained by Germans, and EUTM had not accompanied Malian forces operationally because supporting forces and infrastructure were insufficient. Thus the German nexus to the abusive units remained unverified rather than disproved.","locator":"Answer to question 7, pp. 3-4, PDF lines 122-128; answer to question 20, p. 10, lines 346-351; answer to question 24, pp. 11-12, lines 391-413; answer to question 28, p. 13, lines 439-447.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"mali_2022_german_training_abusive_unit_nexus_unresolved","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5"],"source_type":"primary_government_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed formal answer candidly documents missing traceability data and the absence of tactical accompaniment; these are strong findings about control limitations, though not independent findings of abuse by German-trained personnel.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous June 2022 account of monitoring capacity and knowledge after the reported abuses.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional monitoring deficiency under the Scholz government; no evidence that Scholz personally caused or concealed the missing traceability.","contrary_evidence":"No source-supported link was established between German training and particular Moura perpetrators, vehicles, or units; attribution of the abuses to German assistance would therefore be speculative.","underlying_fact_key":"mali_2022_eutm_trainee_traceability_gap","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Foreign Affairs Council (Defence), 17 May 2022 – Mali operational-training decision","publisher":"Council of the European Union","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/meetings/fac/2022/05/17/","claim":"The Council confirmed the April suspension of operational training for Malian armed-forces and National Guard units because the transitional authorities had not guaranteed non-interference by Russian mercenaries and because of an increasing pattern of collusion and allegations of grave human-rights abuses.","locator":"Meeting-results section concerning EUTM Mali, paragraph beginning “In light of the lack of guarantees from the transition authorities”.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-006","canonical_fact_key":"eu_2022_confirmed_mali_operational_training_suspension","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.9"],"source_type":"primary_multilateral_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official EU Council record independently confirms implementation of the suspension and its stated recipient-risk basis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous confirmation in May 2022 of the April suspension.","ruler_attribution":"Corroborates implementation of the multilateral remedy supported by the Scholz government, but does not independently establish that Scholz personally initiated it.","contrary_evidence":"The Council relied on an increasing pattern and grave allegations plus absent safeguards, not a completed finding that specific EU- or German-trained personnel committed the abuses.","underlying_fact_key":"mali_2022_eutm_suspension_after_abuse_risk","lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The European Union Training Mission in Mali: An Assessment","publisher":"Stockholm International Peace Research Institute","publication_date":"2022-04","url":"https://www.sipri.org/sites/default/files/2022-04/bp_2204_eutm_mali.pdf","claim":"SIPRI’s independent assessment found that EUTM Mali’s ability to evaluate effects after trainees returned to their units was constrained by weak follow-up, limited access, personnel-management problems, and dependence on other missions for operational information. It found some capacity-building impact but no basis for tracing individual German-trained personnel to later misconduct.","locator":"Sections IV-V, “The impact of EUTM Mali” and “Explanatory factors behind the impact of EUTM Mali”; conclusions and recommendations in section VI.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-007","canonical_fact_key":"mali_eutm_independent_effectiveness_and_followup_limits","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5"],"source_type":"independent_research","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dedicated independent effectiveness study by SIPRI researchers, including a Mali-based co-author; it assesses institutional performance while avoiding unsupported perpetrator attribution.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published in April 2022 and assesses EUTM performance through the opening months of the Scholz period.","ruler_attribution":"Context for inherited and continuing mission-control weaknesses; it does not attribute those weaknesses personally to Scholz.","contrary_evidence":"EUTM produced some military capacity-building gains, but the evidence did not demonstrate reliable downstream control, individual traceability, or prevention of misconduct.","underlying_fact_key":"mali_2022_eutm_trainee_traceability_gap","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5"]}
